This instance supplied three sharply different pairs: a 21×21 sparse diagonal-color scene became a full 5-color periodic field; a 23×23 otherwise blank grid with two bottom stripes became an alternating two-color row pattern; and a 2×2 near-uniform patch had its lone zero filled. The 12×12 test instead contained offset diagonal tracks in colors 8, 2, 6, and 7. The demonstrations were evidence of radically different surface transformations, but no cumulative exact-delta analysis produced a validated shared selector or output mapping. Serial demo requests (three were used) did not resolve the relationship; the last action continued requesting rather than making an evidence-based decision.

Reusable lesson: task IDs and stream membership alone do not justify assuming a common pixel-level operation. Record each pair's exact delta and output structure before requesting another. Explicitly test whether the task may be an abstract transformation shared across different visual classes; formulate at least two candidate abstractions and derive predictions that can be replayed against all examples. If no candidate maps all demonstrations exactly, another request is justified only if its predicted outcomes discriminate those candidates. Do not invent a test output by blending the demo patterns or select one demo's operation based on superficial resemblance. Keep the action protocol strict: one JSON action only on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/task-29b1b5eb-recovery.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous edge-extension demos: recovery lesson

In this instance, early demos suggested rightward additions, but their exact patterns differed: a final cell on an almost-full bar, a three-cell right-edge run in a row with other separated runs, an alternating two-row continuation patch, and a two-cell right-edge extension on a bar ending just short of the boundary. The test also contains multiple 9-pattern rows, with interior zero gaps and distinct right-edge endings. A broad rule to replace internal gaps with 1 was submitted and rejected. Switching to suffix-only edits was not validated by exact replay and would be another speculative variant, not a recovery.

For future instances, maintain exact changed-cell masks and unchanged candidate rows after every demo. A shared feature such as “new 1s near the right edge” is insufficient unless a measurable selector accounts for the differing footprint sizes and unchanged rows. Before retrying after rejection, discard the full rule, test alternative task classes/selectors against all pairs, and request another demo only for an explicit discriminator. Same task IDs in historical references are cautionary context, not answer keys.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/task-29b1b5eb-recovery.md"}

